// The running total is over UNBOUNDED elements, so it is a real overflow: the diagnostic
// asks for a bound on the count, the element or the total, and this test supplies none
// of the three because its subject is the loop LOWER bound that makes `a[i]` provable. The addition is therefore
// explicitly wrapping (`+%`), which states that and leaves the subject intact.
// VRA precision: `for i in 0..n` with a non-constant end must still retain the
// known lower bound i >= 0, so a narrowed index proves in-bounds. Previously the
// whole counter range was dropped unless both ends were constant, forcing a
// false E085 "index may be negative".
func f(a i32[10], n i32) i32 {
    var s i32 = 0
    for i in 0..n {
        if i < 10 {
            s = s +% a[i]
        }
    }
    return s
}
func main() i32 {
    return 0
}
